{
  "text": "According to Newton's first law, an object will continue to move at a constant velocity unless acted upon by an external net force. This means that uniform motion will only change if a force is applied. For example, if you're riding a bicycle at a steady speed on a straight road, you'll keep moving forward at that speed. However, if you suddenly hit a bump and the bicycle tilts, this is an external force acting on the bicycle, causing it to change its motion. This change could be a speed increase, decrease, or a direction change, depending on the nature of the force. [forces.txt Page 1]",
  "follow_up_question": "Can you think of another example where an external force changes an object's motion?",
