}

{
  "text": "According to Newton's first law, an object in motion will continue to move at a constant velocity unless acted upon by an external net force. This principle is known as inertia. However, Newton's second law provides a more specific answer to your question. It states that the force acting on an object is equal to its mass multiplied by its acceleration (F = m * a). Therefore, any force that acts on a moving body can cause it to change its velocity. For example, if you're riding a bicycle and suddenly hit the brakes, the force of friction between the brake pads and the wheels slows you down, changing your velocity. This is a practical example of how forces can influence the velocity of a moving body. [forces.txt Page 1]",
